## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of order-statistics trees with duplicates and practical balanced BST design. It's a common systems programming interview topic that combines theoretical data structure knowledge with implementation considerations.

## Key Concepts
- **Augmented BST (treap)**: Each node maintains value, cnt, size, and priority fields
- **Duplicate handling**: Stored via cnt field; size stores the total count including duplicates in the subtree
- **Rank traversal**: Walks the tree, summing counts from left subtrees and comparing with target value
- **Select traversal**: Uses subtree sizes to navigate directly to the k-th element

## How Duplicates Are Stored
The cnt field at each node tracks how many times that value appears. When inserting a duplicate, we simply increment cnt rather than creating a new node. This keeps the tree balanced and efficient.

## How Select/Rank Traverse Equal Keys
- **rank(x)**: When traversing, if current node's value < x, add (left subtree size + cnt) to result and move right. Otherwise, move left. This counts all elements strictly less than x.
- **select(k)**: Navigate by comparing k with left subtree size. If k ≤ left size, go left. If k > left + cnt, subtract (left + cnt) and go right. Otherwise, we've found the node.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(v) {
    this.val = v;
    this.cnt = 1;
    this.size = 1;
    this.prio = Math.random();
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function update(n) {
  if (!n) return;
  n.size = n.cnt + (n.left?.size || 0) + (n.right?.size || 0);
}

function rank(root, x) {
  if (!root) return 0;
  if (x <= root.val) return rank(root.left, x);
  return (root.left?.size || 0) + root.cnt + rank(root.right, x);
}

function select(root, k) {
  if (!root) return null;
  const leftSize = root.left?.size || 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) return select(root.left, k);
  if (k > leftSize + root.cnt) return select(root.right, k - leftSize - root.cnt);
  return root.val;
}
```

